  "textBooks": {
    "yms_year": 114.0,
    "yms_smester": 1.0,
    "cls_id": "CB02320",
    "sub_id": "43541",
    "scr_dup": "001",
    "tpb_seq": 1,
    "tpb_book": null,
    "tpb_book_en": "<div>Pagden, Anthony. (2024). Beyond States: Powers, Peoples and Global Order. Cambridge: Polity.</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>O&rsquo;Neil, Shannon. (2023). The Globalization Myth: Why Regions Matter (Council on Foreign Relations Books). New Haven: Yale University Press.</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>Duszczyk, Maciej, Pachocka, Marta and Pszczolkowska Dominika. (2020). Relations between Immigration and Integration Policies in Europe: Challenges, Opportunities and Perspectives in Selected EU Member States (Routledge Advances in European Politics). New York: Routledge.</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>Taleb, Nicholas. (2010). The Black Swan: The Impact of the Highly Improbable. New York: Random House.</div>\n"
  },
  "readings": {
    "yms_year": 114.0,
    "yms_smester": 1.0,
    "cls_id": "CB02320",
    "sub_id": "43541",
    "scr_dup": "001",
    "tpc_seq": 1,
    "tpc_book": null,
    "tpc_book_en": "<div>These books serve as additional reading to the topics discussed in class. They cover the areas of regionalism in general, globalization, immigration and probability. Exerts from them are going to be used in class for discussions and other activities. It is advisable to follow the readings to better understand the class content.</div>\n"
  },
